    <abstract>
      <sec><title>Background</title><p>Persistent wheeze in children has many causes.</p></sec>
      <sec><title>Results</title><p>Tracheomalacia was found in 31% of 120 children.</p></sec>
    </abstract>
  </article-meta>
</front>
<body>
  <sec id="s1"><title>Background</title>
    <p>Wheeze that persists despite treatment <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R1">[1]</xref> may reflect a structural airway problem such as <italic>tracheomalacia</italic>.</p>
  </sec>
  <sec id="s2"><title>Methods</title>
    <sec id="s2a"><title>Patients</title><p>We included 120 children aged 3 months to 12 years.</p>
      <list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Wheeze for more than 3 months</p></list-item><list-item><p>No known airway disease</p></list-item></list>
    </sec>
  </sec>
  <sec id="s3"><title>Results</title>
    <p>Tracheomalacia was the most frequent finding (31%; 95% CI 23&#x2013;40). See <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://example.org/data">the dataset</ext-link>.</p>
    <fig id="F1"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><title>Bronchoscopic findings.</title><p>Distribution by age.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="12890_2026_1234_Fig1_HTML"/></fig>
    <table-wrap id="T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Findings</p></caption>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>n (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tracheomalacia</td><td>37 (31)</td></tr></tbody></table>
    </table-wrap>
  </sec>
</body>
